claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 09:31:38 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> Claude should treat the claude@submissions as a drafts repo. Any commits
>> in here which have not been integrated into mail@upstream are subject to
>> revision as new information comes in.
>> 
>> /process-email should first look to any un-integrated emails, and see if
>> they need to be revised / removed based on the newest commits.
>
> Done in "[PATCH] claude: Treat unintegrated mail as revisable drafts"
> [1]. Step 1 of process-email now ends by checking every commit in
> mail@upstream..claude against what just arrived. A draft that is out
> of date is revised in place and keeps its Message-ID. A draft that is
> no longer wanted is abandoned. The contributor steps in
> zib/patch-review-workflow.org say the same.
>
>> Contributor should treat this the same as any
>> other conflict with one exception: once a message is in mail@upstream,
>> it's immutable. Conflicting message revisions should be dropped, or
>> integrated into a reply message instead.
>
> The patch handles this with one command. A draft that conflicts with
> the list after the rebase takes the list's version of the file:
>
>     jj restore --from mail@upstream --into <commit> <file>
>
> and is abandoned if that leaves it empty. Anything still worth saying
> goes in a new reply to the integrated message. I tried this on an
> add/add conflict in a scratch repo, and the draft came out empty as
> expected.
>
> There is nothing to revise this pass, because all of my outbox is
> already in mail@upstream. Running this pass also turned up two
> step-1 bugs, and both are already fixed by "[PATCH 0/2] Fix
> process-email's catch-up step" [2]. The bookmark stayed behind on an
> old list, which hid your two newest messages until I moved it. The
> trunk rebase also picked up two mail-branch Justfile commits ("mail:
> Add just archive" and "mail: Add just commit-mail"). I undid that
> rebase and reran it with the series' revset.
>
> [1] id:51d24553357a9c0c014a16e757860da83e6b573a.1791651322.git.claude@p7x.dev
> [2] id:cover.1791648499.git.claude@p7x.dev

I think potentially this went too far... specifically there was a v1
patch, and instead of v2 being posted, it simply rewrote the v1 message
emails. Now there are references to v1, but no actual v1 series.

I don't mind drafts being rewritten based on new information, but that
doesn't apply to new patch series versions.
